//? Title: Zhu–Takaoka Search: When a Pair of Bytes Buys a Longer Shift
//? Subtitle: Safe digram bounds, actual comparison counts and searching Titanic passenger names
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Zhu–Takaoka combines Boyer–Moore's good-suffix rule with a shift indexed by
the final two bytes of a text window. We derive the digram table, including
its easily missed prefix-overlap case, and show why taking the larger of
two safe bounds preserves every occurrence. A compiled matcher searches
`dm::datasets::titanic()` names independently, reports actual byte comparisons,
and exposes each alignment's chosen shift. Naive search and Horspool give
reference results and work counts on the same records. The comparison
includes the algorithm named in the title; preprocessing cost and
periodic worst cases are kept explicit.
)

\toc

> The data: names are separate records

The built-in Titanic dataset contains 891 passengers and 12 columns. Its
`Name` column has surnames, titles, given names and parenthesised names.
This gives natural exact-substring queries with different lengths and
frequencies. Search is case-sensitive on bytes; no linguistic tokenisation
or Unicode normalisation is implied. Each name is searched separately, so
a match cannot run from the end of one passenger's name into the next.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::print("{}\n", passengers.select({"PassengerId", "Name"}).head(8).to_md());
  std::print("\n| records | dataset columns |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} |\n", passengers.nrows(), passengers.ncols());
}
```
