> The method: right-to-left comparison with two safe bounds

For pattern \(P\) of length \(m\ge2\), align its first byte with text
position \(s\). Compare \(P\) against \(T[s,\ldots,s+m-1]\) from
right to left. On a mismatch, inspect the final window digram
\((a,b)=(T[s+m-2],T[s+m-1])\).

Consider a possible occurrence starting \(d>0\) bytes later. If
\(1\le d\le m-2\), the old trailing digram must align with
\((P[m-2-d],P[m-1-d])\). If \(d=m-1\), only the old final byte
remains in the pattern, so it must equal \(P[0]\). A shift of \(m\)
places both old bytes before the candidate. The smallest permitted shift is
therefore

$$
D(a,b)=\min\left(\{m\}
\cup\{m-1:b=P[0]\}
\cup\{m-1-i:1\le i\le m-2,\ (P[i-1],P[i])=(a,b)\}\right).
$$
\alttext(The digram shift is the minimum of the full pattern length, the prefix-overlap shift m minus 1 when the final byte equals the first pattern byte, and distances to internal occurrences of the same digram.)

Initialise every entry to \(m\), set the column for \(P[0]\) to
\(m-1\), then visit internal digrams from left to right, overwriting
entries with decreasing distances. The terminal pattern digram is
excluded: its zero distance would prevent forward progress.

For `Miss.` the internal digrams `Mi`, `is` and `ss` permit shifts 3, 2
and 1. Any pair ending in `M` permits prefix overlap at shift 4. Most
remaining pairs permit shift 5, including `s.`: a candidate with the same
terminal pair still has to move by a positive distance.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("{}\n", dm::algorithms::ZhuTakaoka("Miss.").digram_table("Mis.").to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| first_byte | last_byte | shift |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 77 | 77 | 4 |
| 77 | 105 | 3 |
| 77 | 115 | 5 |
| 77 | 46 | 5 |
| 105 | 77 | 4 |
| 105 | 105 | 5 |
| 105 | 115 | 2 |
| 105 | 46 | 5 |
| 115 | 77 | 4 |
| 115 | 105 | 5 |
| 115 | 115 | 1 |
| 115 | 46 | 5 |
