>> Combining the digram bound with a strong good suffix

Suppose the comparison mismatches at pattern index \(j\), after matching
\(P[j+1,\ldots,m-1]\). The strong good-suffix shift \(G_j\) moves to
the next occurrence of that suffix preceded by a different byte. If none
exists, it aligns the longest possible suffix with a pattern prefix.
Candidates starting before \(G_j\) contradict either the matched suffix
or the mismatching predecessor. Independently, candidates starting before
\(D(a,b)\) contradict the trailing pair. Hence

$$
\Delta=\max\{G_j,D(a,b)\}
$$
\alttext(The chosen shift is the maximum of the strong good-suffix shift and the two-byte shift.)

is safe: any start smaller than the maximum violates at least one necessary
condition. After a full match, use the full-match good-suffix shift, equal
to \(m-\ell\), where \(\ell\) is the length of the longest proper
border of the pattern. This preserves overlapping matches: `aa` in `aaaa`
occurs at offsets 0, 1 and 2.

>> Work and storage

Preprocessing takes \(O(m+\sigma^2)\) time and space for alphabet size
\(\sigma=256\). The digram table has 65 536 integer entries: 256 KiB
when `int` is four bytes. Horspool uses 256 shifts instead. A compiled
`ZhuTakaoka` object pays the digram cost once and reuses it for every record;
the one-shot `zhu_takaoka_search` rebuilds the tables each call.

At most \(n-m+1\) alignments are visited, with up to \(m\) comparisons
at each: the safe worst-case bound is \(O(nm)\). For repeated `a` text
and pattern, every alignment matches and the full-match shift is one, so
this bound is attained. A useful average shift does not prove linear
worst-case complexity or a speed advantage on short records.

For a fixed pattern with \(d\) distinct internal digrams, an independently
uniform window-end pair over \(\sigma\) symbols is absent from that set
with probability exactly \(1-d/\sigma^2\). Absent pairs usually license
long shifts, subject to prefix overlap. Passenger names are structured,
and visited window ends are selected by previous shifts, so this toy
probability is not a performance prediction for this dataset.

> Following an actual search through one name

The third name contains `Miss.`. The trace is produced by the same search
loop used by `search` and `scan`. It counts successful byte equality tests
and the failed test ending an alignment, while excluding size checks and
preprocessing. A complete match uses only the good-suffix shift, so its
reported digram contribution is zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zhu_takaoka_shifts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic();
  const auto& name = passengers.string_at("Name", 2);
  const dm::algorithms::ZhuTakaoka matcher("Miss.");
  std::print("{}\n\n", name);
  const auto trace = matcher.trace(name);
  std::print("{}\n", trace.to_md());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(trace.get_column("offset").values(), trace.get_column("advance").values(),
            "selected advance", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253})
      .points(trace.get_column("offset").values(), trace.get_column("digram_shift").values(),
              "digram bound", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .points(trace.get_column("offset").values(), trace.get_column("suffix_shift").values(),
              "suffix bound", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 4)
      .title("Which shift wins at each visited name window?")
      .x_label("alignment offset in bytes").y_label("advance in bytes")
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/zhu_takaoka_shifts.svg");
}
```
