| 11 | 5 | 5 | 1 | 0 | 5 | 5 |
| 16 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 5 | 1 | 5 |

\image(figures/zhu_takaoka_shifts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual alignment trace on a passenger name, showing the digram bound, suffix bound and selected advance.)
\alttext(A bar chart of selected shifts at visited byte offsets, with points for the digram and suffix bounds. Each selected shift equals the larger applicable bound.)

> Comparing actual scans over the complete dataset

The corpus helper precomputes each pattern's tables once and searches all
891 records. It reports counts from naive left-to-right search, Horspool,
and Zhu–Takaoka, comparing their complete position vectors record by
record. `agrees_with_naive=1` confirms this comparison, not just equality
of aggregate match totals. These are exact substring queries: `William`
can occur in a given name, a parenthesised name or a surname.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zhu_takaoka_comparisons.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto comparison = dm::algorithms::string_search_comparison(
      dm::datasets::titanic(), "Name", {"Mr.", "Mrs.", "Miss.", "Master.", "William"});
  std::print("{}\n", comparison.to_md());
  const auto totals = comparison.select({"method", "comparisons", "alignments"}) |
      dm::group_by("method") | dm::summarise_each(dm::sum);
  std::print("\n{}\n", totals.to_md());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(totals.get_column("comparisons").values(),
                             totals.get_column<std::string>("method").values(), "byte comparisons")
      .title("Five queries over the same 891 passenger names")
      .y_label("total scan comparisons, excluding preprocessing")
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/zhu_takaoka_comparisons.svg");
}
```
