\image(figures/zhu_takaoka_comparisons.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual match, alignment and comparison counts for all three methods, followed by totals for the five queries. Every method searches identical unmodified records.)
\alttext(A bar chart comparing total byte-equality tests by naive search, Horspool and Zhu–Takaoka across five passenger-name queries.)

All 15 method/query combinations agree record by record. Across the five
queries, naive search performs 108 699 comparisons, Horspool 30 351 and
Zhu–Takaoka 27 981. The digram rule saves about 7.8% relative to Horspool
on these records. Its advantage is a measured property of these queries,
not a guarantee for every name corpus or pattern.

Counts exclude allocation and table construction, and are not elapsed-time
benchmarks. The larger digram table can lose on cache or setup costs even
when it saves comparisons. Reusing a matcher is particularly relevant for
many short strings such as these names.

> Testing the periodic limit

The worst-case construction needs no random generator. For pattern
`aaaa` in `aaaaaaaa`, every possible alignment matches, all four bytes
are compared, and the period-one good-suffix shift advances by one.
This diagnostic is a theoretical counterexample, separate from the dataset
analysis.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto result = dm::algorithms::ZhuTakaoka("aaaa").scan("aaaaaaaa");
  std::print("| alignments | matches | comparisons | m(n-m+1) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
             "| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", result.alignments, result.positions.size(), result.comparisons, 4*(8-4+1));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| alignments | matches | comparisons | m(n-m+1) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 5 | 20 | 20 |
// result_end
\caption(The repeated-byte case attains m times n minus m plus one comparisons, while preserving every overlapping match.)

> Conclusion

A two-byte shift supplies a stronger necessary condition than a single
byte in many windows. The prefix-overlap case and strong good-suffix rule
are both needed to move safely. Actual instrumented scans show whether
that extra information reduces work on these passenger names; the trace
explains each chosen advance. The larger preprocessing table and periodic
worst case remain relevant even when the comparison chart is favourable.

> References

- R. F. Zhu and T. Takaoka (1987). *On improving the average case of the Boyer–Moore string matching algorithm*.
  Journal of Information Processing 10(3), 173–177. https://ipsj.ixsq.nii.ac.jp/records/59825
- R. N. Horspool (1980). *Practical fast searching in strings*.
  Software: Practice and Experience 10(6), 501–506. https://doi.org/10.1002/spe.4380100608
- C. Charras and T. Lecroq. *Exact String Matching Algorithms: Zhu–Takaoka algorithm*.
  https://www-igm.univ-mlv.fr/~lecroq/string/node20.html
